#horizontalrule

== Upon Whom is Qurbānī Obligatory?
<upon-whom-is-qurbani-obligatory>
=== Basic Criteria (for all wājibāt)
<basic-criteria-for-all-wajibat>
The person must be: 1. #strong[Muslim] 2. #strong[ʿĀqil] (sane) 3.
#strong[Bāligh] (mature)

=== Specific Criteria for Qurbānī
<specific-criteria-for-qurbani>
In addition to the above: 4. #strong[Muqīm] (resident, not a
musāfir/traveler) 5. #strong[Ṣāḥib al-Niṣāb] --- owner of the niṣāb
amount beyond basic needs (#strong[ḥājāt aṣliyyah])

The niṣāb is the value of #strong[612+ grams of silver] (the lower of
the two niṣāb thresholds of gold and silver).

#horizontalrule

=== Two Key Differences from Zakāt Niṣāb
<two-key-differences-from-zakat-nisab>
#figure(
  align(center)[#table(
    columns: (17.07%, 17.07%, 65.85%),
    align: (auto,auto,auto,),
    table.header([Point], [Zakāt], [Qurbānī / Ṣadaqat al-Fiṭr],),
    table.hline(),
    [#strong[Ḥawl (completion of year)]], [Required --- niṣāb must be
    held for one full year], [#strong[Not required] --- even if one
    acquires the niṣāb on the last day of Eid before sunset, Qurbānī
    becomes wājib],
    [#strong[Exemptions for personal-use items]], [Broader --- e.g., a
    backup car (not for trade) is #strong[not] counted in
    niṣāb], [#strong[Narrower] --- a backup car, extra house not in use,
    etc. #strong[are] counted toward niṣāb],
  )]
  , kind: table
  )

#strong[Example:] If a person has two houses --- one in use, one as
backup --- the value of the backup house is counted in the Qurbānī niṣāb
(but not in Zakāt niṣāb).

#horizontalrule

=== Women and Qurbānī
<women-and-qurbani>
Qurbānī is #strong[individually obligatory] upon women who meet the
niṣāb, even if their only asset is jewelry.

#strong[Niṣāb calculation for women:] - If she has #strong[only gold]
(no silver, no cash, no other assets): the #strong[niṣāb of gold] must
be completed. - If she has a #strong[mixture] (gold + some cash, or gold
\+ silver): the #strong[niṣāb of silver] (the lower threshold) is used
--- the combined value is assessed against 612g of silver.

#strong[Practical advice:] Women should plan for Qurbānī throughout the
year (e.g., saving a small amount daily), rather than waking up on Eid
day wondering how to arrange funds. The husband commonly takes this
burden upon himself, which is permissible --- but it must be done
#strong[with the wife's knowledge].

#horizontalrule

=== One Qurbānī per Person, Not per Family
<one-qurbani-per-person-not-per-family>
A common misconception is that one Qurbānī suffices for the entire
family. This is #strong[incorrect] in the Ḥanafī school. Every
individual who is Muslim, ʿāqil, bāligh, muqīm, and ṣāḥib al-niṣāb must
have their own Qurbānī performed.

#horizontalrule

=== Debt and Niṣāb
<debt-and-nisab>
If a person is under debt, the debt amount is #strong[deducted] from
their total assets. If the remainder still meets the niṣāb, Qurbānī is
wājib.

#strong[For long-term loans (home loans, etc.):] Only the #strong[yearly
installment due] is deducted --- not the entire loan amount. Otherwise,
most wealthy business owners with large commercial loans would fall
below the niṣāb, which defeats the purpose.

#strong[Example:] A person has \$10,000 in assets and a home loan with a
\$6,000 annual installment. Only \$6,000 is deducted → \$4,000 remaining
→ check if this meets the niṣāb.

#horizontalrule

=== No Qurbānī on Immature Children or Insane Persons
<no-qurbani-on-immature-children-or-insane-persons>
- There is #strong[no] Qurbānī obligation on an #strong[immature child]
  (not bāligh), regardless of their wealth.
- There is #strong[no] Qurbānī obligation on a #strong[majnūn] (insane
  person).
- However, it is #strong[recommended] for a wealthy father to perform
  Qurbānī on behalf of his immature children.

=== Poor Person Who Buys an Animal
<poor-person-who-buys-an-animal>
If a poor person (not ṣāḥib al-niṣāb) #strong[purchases an animal with
the intention of Qurbānī], the Qurbānī becomes #strong[wājib] upon him
on account of that purchase.

#horizontalrule

== Days and Timing of Qurbānī
<days-and-timing-of-qurbani>
=== Three Days
<three-days>
The days of slaughtering are the #strong[10th, 11th, and 12th of Dhul
Ḥijjah] --- three days. The first day is the best.

- Slaughtering at #strong[night] is #strong[makrūh] (the Prophet ﷺ did
  not do it).
- The time technically begins after #strong[Fajr] on the 10th (Sharʿī
  daytime begins at Fajr, not sunrise).

=== In Cities and Large Villages (Where Eid Prayer is Performed)
<in-cities-and-large-villages-where-eid-prayer-is-performed>
Slaughtering #strong[must be done after the Eid prayer]. If done before
the prayer, the Qurbānī is #strong[not valid] and must be
#strong[repeated] (the meat is still ḥalāl, but the wājib is not
fulfilled).

#strong[If there are multiple masjids:] Once the first masjid in the
city completes its Eid prayer, slaughtering becomes permissible for the
entire city.

=== In Small Villages (Where No Eid Prayer is Performed)
<in-small-villages-where-no-eid-prayer-is-performed>
Slaughtering may be done immediately #strong[after Fajr] --- no need to
wait for any Eid prayer.

#strong[Practical shortcut:] A person in the city who wants to eat early
can send his animal to a nearby village where no Eid prayer is
performed. The animal can be slaughtered after Fajr there, then brought
back to the city. The #strong[place of the animal] is what determines
the ruling, not the person's location.

#horizontalrule

== Sending Qurbānī Abroad
<sending-qurbani-abroad>
When Qurbānī is sent to another country (e.g., from the US to
India/Pakistan), it must be performed on a #strong[common Eid day] --- a
day that is an Eid day in #strong[both] locations:

- The place of the #strong[animal] and the place of the #strong[person]
  must both be on a valid Eid day.
- If Eid in the US is Wednesday and Eid in India is Thursday, the
  Qurbānī in India must be done on #strong[Thursday] (not Wednesday),
  because Wednesday is not yet Eid in India.
- It should #strong[not] be done on a day that would be the 13th in the
  person's location (as that is beyond the valid days).

#horizontalrule

== Charity Cannot Replace Qurbānī
<charity-cannot-replace-qurbani>
Some people argue that slaughtering is a "waste of money" and that the
amount should be given as charity instead. This is #strong[incorrect].
ʿIbādāt are fixed by Allah's command, not by human logic. No ṣadaqah can
replace Qurbānī.

#strong[Additional benefits of Qurbānī] (beyond the primary purpose of
ʿibādah and remembering Ibrāhīm ʿalayhi al-salām): - Boosts the local
economy where mass slaughtering occurs. - Provides the best source of
protein to poor people who otherwise cannot afford meat.

#horizontalrule

== Animals for Qurbānī
<animals-for-qurbani>
Three categories of animals are valid:

#figure(
  align(center)[#table(
    columns: (37.04%, 33.33%, 29.63%),
    align: (auto,auto,auto,),
    table.header([Category], [Animals], [Shares],),
    table.hline(),
    [#strong[Small animals]], [Goat, sheep, ram, lamb], [#strong[1
    share] (1 animal = 1 person)],
    [#strong[Cattle]], [Cow, buffalo, ox], [#strong[Up to 7 shares]],
    [#strong[Camel]], [Camel], [#strong[Up to 7 shares]],
  )]
  , kind: table
  )

#strong[Note on buffalo:] Some groups object that buffalo is not
mentioned in ḥadīth regarding Eid slaughtering. The Ḥanafī scholars have
addressed this --- the teacher will share the relevant pages from
Mawlānā Yūsuf Ludhyānwī's work on this topic.

#strong[Wild/pet animals:] Only domesticated livestock of the above
types are valid. Wild animals --- even if kept as pets --- are
#strong[not valid] for Qurbānī.

#horizontalrule

== Partnerships in Larger Animals --- Critical Rulings
<partnerships-in-larger-animals-critical-rulings>
When sharing a cow, buffalo, or camel among up to seven partners:

=== All Partners Must Intend ʿIbādah
<all-partners-must-intend-ibadah>
Every partner's share must be for an act of #strong[ʿibādah] performed
through slaughtering --- whether Qurbānī, Walīmah, ʿAqīqah, or similar.
If even #strong[one partner] has no ʿibādah intention (just wants meat),
the Qurbānī of #strong[all partners] is invalidated.

=== The Ḥarām Income Problem
<the-haram-income-problem>
This is a very sensitive and critical case:

- If one partner's income is #strong[majority ḥarām], and he pays his
  share from ḥarām earnings, the Qurbānī is #strong[invalid for all
  partners].
- If there is #strong[no evidence] against a partner's income, the
  default assumption is that his earning is #strong[ḥalāl] --- benefit
  of the doubt applies.
- Assessment is based on #strong[ẓāhir] (apparent situation) --- what is
  observed and known about the person's lifestyle and sources of income.
- You are #strong[not required] to investigate deeply, but you
  #strong[must not knowingly] partner with someone whose majority income
  is ḥarām.

#strong[Practical advice:] Choose partners who are personally known to
you and whose ḥalāl earning you are reasonably confident about. When
using Qurbānī services where partners are assigned by the organization,
the benefit of doubt applies --- as long as you have no specific
evidence of a problem.

#strong[Mixed income (e.g., gas station selling both ḥalāl items and
ḥarām items like alcohol/lottery):] The #strong[majority] of their
income is assessed. If the majority comes from ḥalāl sources (gas, food,
etc.), benefit of doubt is given.

#horizontalrule

== Q&A from Students
<qa-from-students>
#strong[Q:] In the US, many people do one Qurbānī per family even when
multiple family members are ṣāḥib al-niṣāb. Is this valid in any
madhhab?

#strong[A (Teacher):] In the Ḥanafī school, this is clearly not valid
--- it is per person. The teacher will verify the position of other
schools.

#horizontalrule

#strong[Q:] Hair trimming --- if the moustache grows over the lip during
these ten days and interferes with eating, can it be trimmed?

#strong[A (Teacher):] Yes. The prohibition of cutting hair/nails is only
a #strong[recommendation]. If it causes difficulty, removing the harm
takes priority. The principle: #emph["Removing harm is preferred over
gaining benefit"] (dar' al-mafāsid muqaddam ʿalā jalb al-manāfiʿ).

